in general ncis is not the kind of show  that carries plot lines over from week  to week  the series has a crime procedural and as  such there's typically a new case each  week that the team has to solve  while stories may not carry over through  successive episodes  certain character details do remain  constant we're not suddenly going to see  special agent leroy jethro gibbs start  giving soliloquies any time  soon the leader and former marine  gunnery sergeant is going to remain a  man of few words  and let his piercing gays do most of the  talking it's his personality  has shaped through the life he's lived  before and during his time on the major  case response team  these details are subtle reminders of  how the show's central players have been  changed by the things they've gone  through and they reinforce the idea that  while the team is always taking new  cases  the characters don't forget all the  things they've been through together  the same can be said for everyone's  favorite wilshere return or not  character  ziva david played by cody de pablo her  prior life and her time alongside the  ntis team has shaped her personality  in fact one case in particular had a  noticeable impact on her wardrobe  the case in question was investigated in  the 16th episode of ntis  season 4 entitled dead man walking this  ncis episode featured a navy lieutenant  named royce sanders  who shows up in the mcrt bullpen and  isn't looking too good  he's coming to the team with a simple  request to solve a murder  his own murder in fact no he's not a  ghost visiting them from beyond  the grave as he demonstrates by removing  a clump of hair  roy has realized he's been poisoned with  radiation and will meet an untimely end  it turns out he works at the  international atomic energy agency  but doesn't believe his sickness is mere  occupational hazard  the team manages to suss out how roy's  been poisoned abby isolates radioactive  ash from a shooting range  and ducky posits that the thalium has  been laced into roy's cigars but the  team can't seem to figure out a motive  that is until gibbs puts together that  roy is due to go to uzbekistan to  inspect a nuclear reactor  given that it made a similar trip six  times previously  it's likely that he would have noticed  the changes that have been made recently  changes that appear to be geared toward  re-establishing a soviet-era weapons  program at the site  all it takes is a 50 000 bribe to iaea  admin holly stegmann for her to poison  roy sadly her goal was to simply prevent  him from making the inspection trip  and she's dismayed to learn he's dying  roy sanders and zeva david form a close  bond over the course of the episode  in part because eva actually recognizes  him but can't figure out why at first  tony denozzo wonders if it could be from  her past with mossad  eventually she realizes that she's seen  him running and the two bond over having  similar fathers and other life  experiences  i pass you every morning i'm going east  you're going west don't you recognize me  the ncis team is ultimately able to  solve roy's murder  but he eventually succumbs to the  thalian poisoning he already knew would  kill him  the navy lieutenant doesn't survive the  episode but before he dies  the bond he forms with zeva clearly  makes a lasting impression on her  one that would carry over into  subsequent episodes after roy's death in  dead man walking  zebra is seen wearing an orange beanie  some fans believe that it's actually  roy's old running attire  you're the one who wears the fluorescent  orange watch cap it's a devastating  episode  in part because roy had quickly  established himself as a likable  character  and as someone with whom ziva had a real  connection the fact that zeva seems to  wear his beanie following his death  speaks to that connection and to the  fact that although the ncis team has  moved on to other cases  the show's characters still remember the  cases that had the biggest impact on  them  that orange beanie became a regular part  of zeva's identity  and although it's a subtle detail it's  one that fans on reddit noticed and  appreciated for what it represented  every ncis character develops their own  history as a result of the cases the  team takes on  even if they're not always explicitly  acknowledged check out one of our newest  videos right here plus  even more looper videos about ntis are  coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

